<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Determining Reasonable Inputs and Outputs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01072</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Determining Reasonable Inputs and Outputs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1ec08e58-b14c-49d6-91d6-e7048c1bda17</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001" class="numbered-exercises"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Earlier, you saw a situation where the total number of times a dog has barked was a function of the time, in seconds, after its owner tied its leash to a post and left. Less than 3 minutes after he left, the owner returned, untied the leash, and walked away with the dog.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Could each value be an input of the function? Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
        
        <para id="para-00003">a. 15</para>
                <para id="para-00004">b. <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>84</m:mn><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00011">c. 300</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">a. Yes</para>
                 <para id="para-00010">b. Yes</para>
          <para id="para-00014">c. No. The owner returned in under 180 seconds and then walked away with the dog, so 300 seconds is not a possible input (assuming that the function is limited to the time the dog was tied to the leash and when the owner was away).</para>
 </solution>
      </exercise>
    
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Could each value be an output of the function? Be prepared to explain your reasoning.
          </para>
        <para id="para-00016">a. 15</para>
              <para id="para-00007">b. <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>84</m:mn><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00024">c. 300</para>


        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00019">a. Yes</para>
          <para id="para-00023">b. No. It is not possible for the dog to bark a fractional number of times.</para>
          <para id="para-00027">c. Yes</para>


        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Building Character: Creativity</title>
          
            <media alt="Illustration of a person sitting and thinking, surrounded by creative tools like a pencil, paint swatches, pen, laptop, light bulb, and papers, symbolizing brainstorming or artistic inspiration.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_15_02_01.png" width="200"/>
            </media>
          
          
            <para id="para-00029">Despite the stereotype of the mad genius, everyday creativity is just as important. You can use it to help you improve your mood and cope with stress, feel more connected with other people and the world—and even help you find meaning and purpose in life.</para>
            <para id="para-00030">Think about your current sense of creativity. Are the following statements true for you?</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
              <item>
          I often make decisions that take me outside my comfort zone.
       </item>
              <item>
          I have a playful attitude toward learning something new.
       </item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00031">Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
          
        
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>